{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790721651,"nonce":"9OfJCfdP0hojsbPAjeTtXOq8Hsw=","title":"Keep deferred snapshot decoding off timed first calls","message":"On-demand snapshot bodies reduce process startup, but a short kernel can begin its timer before the first library helper is decoded and compiled.\n\nNUC x86_64, LLVM 21, `any_field`, 41 interleaved process runs comparing 351efdd56294e50965b1aaf76e68c63ec250b2af with 2a0e3a1dca394c68b9e816bffb83fed7224b0f3e:\n\n- process wall: 25.417 ms (23.365..29.266) to 23.300 ms (20.405..25.975), 8.3% lower\n- benchmark elapsed: 1.648 ms (0.744..2.628) to 2.275 ms (1.390..2.964), 38% higher\n- both print `result: 1500000`\n\nThe startup saving is real, but part of the work moved inside the benchmark timer. Measure where first-demand decode and compile run. Likely levers are reading direct-call prefetch from the split directory instead of decoding the body once before the optimizer decodes it, decoding static-hot functions on the warm-up worker, and ensuring the executing interpreter never waits for a body a worker can prepare.","files":null}]}